WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.298
Where does the data of a FundsXML file live once it has arrived?

2
00:00:04.348 --> 00:00:18.666
In this film you see two ways of storing FundsXML in a database, a complete table model in which every repeating part of a file has its place, the traps around keys and currencies, and how to keep the history of deliveries and corrections.

3
00:00:18.716 --> 00:00:23.956
Everything you see comes from a complete example that you can download and run yourself.

4
00:00:25.206 --> 00:00:26.911
There are two basic ways.

5
00:00:26.961 --> 00:00:30.773
The first: store the whole document in an X M L column.

6
00:00:30.823 --> 00:00:36.530
PostgreSQL, Oracle and SQL Server all have one, and they can query it with X Path.

7
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.304
The second: shred the document into tables, one per business object.

8
00:00:41.354 --> 00:00:47.974
Most setups do both: they keep the original file, and they load the data they need into tables.

9
00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:54.342
Storing the document is simple and keeps everything, also data you do not use today.

10
00:00:54.392 --> 00:01:03.873
In the example, the deliveries table keeps every file in an X M L column, and a query counts the funds, subfunds and prices of each file directly with X Path.

11
00:01:03.923 --> 00:01:10.087
That is good for archives and for occasional questions, but slow for reporting over thousands of files.

12
00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:17.192
Some teams convert the file to J S O N and store that instead, because web applications like it.

13
00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:24.388
That works, but the conversion loses details: the order of elements and the difference between attributes and elements.

14
00:01:24.438 --> 00:01:30.737
Whatever you choose for daily use, keep the original file, unchanged, as the record of what was received.

15
00:01:32.087 --> 00:01:36.212
Many setups arrange the database in layers, and so does the example.

16
00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:39.282
The raw layer holds the original files, unchanged.

17
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:45.606
The staging layer holds data as delivered, still unchecked: here, positions that wait for their assets.

18
00:01:45.656 --> 00:01:49.781
The core layer holds checked data with its own keys and the history.

19
00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:54.816
And the reporting layer offers views for the people and systems that use the data.

20
00:01:54.866 --> 00:01:59.114
Each layer has one job, and errors stay in the layer where they occur.

21
00:02:00.464 --> 00:02:02.657
For daily work, tables are better.

22
00:02:02.707 --> 00:02:06.551
The model of the example follows the structure of FundsXML.

23
00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.860
Every delivery is registered first.

24
00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:24.169
Then come funds and, for umbrellas, their subfunds; share classes with their prices and net asset values; portfolios with their positions; the assets the positions refer to; the transactions; documents; and the regulatory templates.

25
00:02:24.219 --> 00:02:26.610
Twenty tables in all, in eight files.

26
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.768
Here is the table definition.

27
00:02:29.818 --> 00:02:41.047
The columns follow the FundsXML elements, so that the mapping stays easy to read: the fund has its L E I, its name, its currency and, for a subfund, the umbrella it belongs to.

28
00:02:41.097 --> 00:02:43.930
The share class has its I S I N and the fund.

29
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:50.915
A price row has the share class, the date, the nature of the price, the value, and the delivery it came from.

30
00:02:52.265 --> 00:02:58.348
The most important rule of the model: everything that can repeat in a file is a row, never a column.

31
00:02:58.398 --> 00:03:00.249
A file can carry several funds.

32
00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:02.273
A fund has several share classes.

33
00:03:02.323 --> 00:03:07.916
A share class has prices for many dates, and on one date an estimated and an official price.

34
00:03:07.966 --> 00:03:10.247
Net assets come in several currencies.

35
00:03:10.297 --> 00:03:14.540
A fund has portfolios for many dates, each with hundreds of positions.

36
00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:20.857
Whoever puts a second date or a second currency into columns rebuilds the table with the next delivery.

37
00:03:22.207 --> 00:03:24.686
Here is what the example deliveries bring.

38
00:03:24.736 --> 00:03:31.972
The month-end file holds four funds: an umbrella with two subfunds, and two single funds, so five rows in the fund table.

39
00:03:32.022 --> 00:03:39.558
Together that is twenty-four valuation days, two hundred sixty-four prices and five hundred twenty-two positions, in one file.

40
00:03:39.608 --> 00:03:44.315
The daily file brings three days, each with an estimated and an official value.

41
00:03:45.665 --> 00:03:47.893
One share class on one day shows why.

42
00:03:47.943 --> 00:03:59.407
The franc class of the multi-asset fund reports its net assets as an estimate and as the official value, each in francs and in euros, flagged as share-class currency and as fund currency.

43
00:03:59.457 --> 00:04:01.624
Four rows, one day, one share class.

44
00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:05.195
A table with one value column per day could not hold them.

45
00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:09.112
Here is one position on its way into a table.

46
00:04:09.162 --> 00:04:15.624
At the top, in the file: the sender's reference to the asset, the total value with its currency, and the weight.

47
00:04:15.674 --> 00:04:25.392
Below, the row: the asset is now the key of the database; the value keeps its currency; the row knows its portfolio, and through it the fund, the date and the delivery.

48
00:04:25.442 --> 00:04:31.439
And it keeps its number in the file, so that a generator can write the positions back in the same order.

49
00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:37.469
A fund of funds can also deliver what its target funds hold: the look-through.

50
00:04:37.519 --> 00:04:43.471
These positions have the same shape as the fund's own, so they go into the same table, with a flag.

51
00:04:43.521 --> 00:04:45.776
The example has seventy-three of them.

52
00:04:45.826 --> 00:04:52.203
The flag matters: the reconciliation counts only the fund's own positions, never the look-through as well.

53
00:04:53.553 --> 00:04:56.520
One structural point: single funds and umbrellas.

54
00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:04.772
FundsXML says with the single fund flag whether a fund has share classes directly, or subfunds that each have their own share classes.

55
00:05:04.822 --> 00:05:09.206
In the database, one fund table with a parent column handles both cases.

56
00:05:09.256 --> 00:05:18.320
In the example, the umbrella and its two subfunds are three rows of the same table, and queries do not need to know which kind of fund they look at.

57
00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.119
One trap concerns keys.

58
00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:24.575
A position points to its asset through the Unique ID.

59
00:05:24.625 --> 00:05:31.225
But that ID is only valid inside one file: the next delivery may use the same ID for another security.

60
00:05:31.275 --> 00:05:41.395
So the database needs its own keys, based on the ISIN, the LEI or the sender's own codes, and the link between position and asset is resolved while loading.

61
00:05:42.745 --> 00:05:44.759
And not every asset has an I S I N.

62
00:05:44.809 --> 00:05:53.135
Cash accounts, over-the-counter derivatives and currency forwards usually have none; in the example, forty-five of four hundred thirty assets.

63
00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:58.385
For them, build a key from the sender, the type of asset and the sender's own identifier.

64
00:05:58.435 --> 00:06:04.342
Keep all identifiers of an asset in a table of their own, so that any of them can be used to find it.

65
00:06:05.692 --> 00:06:08.798
Amounts are the next trap, as the franc class showed.

66
00:06:08.848 --> 00:06:16.538
In FundsXML, one amount can be given in several currencies at once, with flags for the fund currency and the share-class currency.

67
00:06:16.588 --> 00:06:23.028
So the database stores the currency with every amount, keeps the flags, and writes several rows where needed.

68
00:06:23.078 --> 00:06:27.672
A bare number without its currency is the most common source of wrong reports.

69
00:06:29.022 --> 00:06:35.864
Many FundsXML elements use fixed lists of codes: document types, price natures, data operations and many more.

70
00:06:35.914 --> 00:06:41.690
Store them in lookup tables, filled from the schema, and refresh them with every new version.

71
00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:49.647
In the example, a small script reads the lists from the schema file and writes them into a code list table, per schema version.

72
00:06:49.697 --> 00:06:54.659
Then the database can check incoming codes, and reports can show readable names.

73
00:06:56.009 --> 00:06:57.250
Dates need care too.

74
00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.772
A file has a content date, the day its data is valid for, and a generation time, when it was written.

75
00:07:03.822 --> 00:07:06.614
A price has its own date, the valuation day.

76
00:07:06.664 --> 00:07:11.909
These can all differ: a file generated on Monday morning carries prices of Friday.

77
00:07:11.959 --> 00:07:16.106
Store all of them, and decide clearly which one your reports use.

78
00:07:17.456 --> 00:07:22.055
Transactions live in four places in FundsXML, and the model keeps them apart.

79
00:07:22.105 --> 00:07:26.222
Subscriptions, redemptions and distributions belong to a share class.

80
00:07:26.272 --> 00:07:30.509
The fund's own trades and its income belong to the portfolio of a date.

81
00:07:30.559 --> 00:07:38.721
The month-end file of the example brings sixty-seven flows, three distributions, two hundred nine trades and sixty-eight income records.

82
00:07:38.771 --> 00:07:44.276
Trades keep their complete X M L next to the columns, so nothing of a complex trade is lost.

83
00:07:45.626 --> 00:07:47.702
Documents get a table of their own.

84
00:07:47.752 --> 00:07:58.330
A document is either a link, then the table keeps the address, or it is embedded, then the table keeps the decoded file itself, with its size and a hash to recognise it again.

85
00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:07.986
A second table says which funds and share classes a document belongs to; one key information document can apply to several share classes, in several languages.

86
00:08:08.036 --> 00:08:11.205
A function returns the documents in force on any day.

87
00:08:12.555 --> 00:08:19.232
Regulatory templates are a world of their own: hundreds of fields each, and a new version every few years.

88
00:08:19.282 --> 00:08:34.718
The example keeps every template record in one table: the template, its version, the share class, the record as X M L, and the few fields it reports on as columns, such as the ongoing costs of the E M T or the S F D R product type of the E E T.

89
00:08:34.768 --> 00:08:38.589
Avoid one column per field and version; it ends in confusion.

90
00:08:39.939 --> 00:08:42.239
Data changes, and corrections arrive.

91
00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:50.304
So every row remembers where it came from: the delivery, its document ID, and the content date, the date the data is valid for.

92
00:08:50.354 --> 00:08:56.654
A delivery with the data operation amend replaces the values of an earlier one; delete removes them.

93
00:08:56.704 --> 00:09:00.909
The original rows are not overwritten, they are marked as replaced.

94
00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:13.233
This makes questions like these simple: what was the net asset value of the franc class on the fourth of November, as known today, and as known on the morning of the sixth, before the correction arrived?

95
00:09:13.283 --> 00:09:15.025
The correction changed one value.

96
00:09:15.075 --> 00:09:19.098
Both versions are in the table, and the query picks the one that was valid.

97
00:09:20.448 --> 00:09:23.421
Users should not have to know how the history works.

98
00:09:23.471 --> 00:09:30.629
Offer views for each use: the current prices, the current net assets, the latest portfolio per fund, the documents in force.

99
00:09:30.679 --> 00:09:35.861
The views hide the logic of deliveries and replaced rows, and give consumers stable names.

100
00:09:35.911 --> 00:09:40.977
Then you can change the tables underneath without breaking every report that reads them.

101
00:09:42.327 --> 00:09:44.961
Store the quality of the data with the data.

102
00:09:45.011 --> 00:09:48.377
Each delivery has a status: loaded, checked or released.

103
00:09:48.427 --> 00:09:54.232
The findings of the checks are stored with it: the rule, the place in the file, the value found.

104
00:09:54.282 --> 00:09:58.624
A function releases a checked delivery and records who did it, and when.

105
00:09:58.674 --> 00:10:03.504
The views show released data only, and anyone can see why a value was held back.

106
00:10:04.854 --> 00:10:06.507
Portfolios are confidential.

107
00:10:06.557 --> 00:10:13.928
A data vendor or an insurer may hold the portfolios of many clients in one database, and each client may only see its own.

108
00:10:13.978 --> 00:10:19.220
So separate the roles of loading, releasing and reading, and control access per client.

109
00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:26.823
The example does it with row-level security: a reader for the insurer sees the one fund its client may see, and nothing else.

110
00:10:28.173 --> 00:10:30.030
A few words on performance.

111
00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.773
Positions grow fastest: thousands per fund and month.

112
00:10:33.823 --> 00:10:37.798
Index the columns you search by: ISIN, date and delivery.

113
00:10:37.848 --> 00:10:40.058
Partition large tables by month.

114
00:10:40.108 --> 00:10:42.812
And load in bulk instead of row by row.

115
00:10:42.862 --> 00:10:49.662
With these measures, ordinary databases handle many years of FundsXML deliveries without trouble.

116
00:10:51.012 --> 00:10:54.560
A last piece of advice: do not model the whole schema at once.

117
00:10:54.610 --> 00:11:00.886
Start with the data you really use, keep the original file next to it, and add tables when new needs come up.

118
00:11:00.936 --> 00:11:05.645
The schema has thousands of elements, and most companies use a small part of them.

119
00:11:06.995 --> 00:11:18.681
Everything you saw in this film is in the complete example: the table definitions, the views and the access rules, the code lists, the sample deliveries, the loader and the generator.

120
00:11:18.731 --> 00:11:26.504
You find it in the FundsXML examples repository on GitHub, in the folder PostgreSQL end to end, also as a single zip file.

121
00:11:26.554 --> 00:11:31.763
One script builds the database, loads the files and runs every query of this film.

122
00:11:33.113 --> 00:11:41.452
That was FundsXML in a database: documents and tables, everything that repeats as rows, keys, amounts and codes, and history.

123
00:11:41.502 --> 00:11:45.881
In the next film, you load the example deliveries into this model.

124
00:11:45.931 --> 00:11:47.223
Thanks for watching.
